.TH TIMECRATE 1 "2026-10-28" "timecrate 3.1.1" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
timecrate \- encrypted, signed, off\-site disaster\-recovery backup
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B timecrate
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B timecrate
ships the irreplaceable slice of a machine \(em configuration, secrets, identity, hand\-tuned
state \(em to a cloud remote as a single compressed, encrypted and
.B signed
archive called a
.IR kit .
Bulk that can be reinstalled or re\-downloaded is deliberately left out; a manifest of installed
packages, enabled units and desktop settings rides along instead and is replayed at restore.
.PP
A kit is built with
.BR tar (1),
compressed with
.BR zstd (1)
and encrypted to a GPG public key with an AES\-256 session cipher. It is also signed by a
standalone key, and every path that extracts a kit verifies that signature
.B before
writing a single byte \(em a kit that is not signed by the expected key is refused, because
anyone who can write to the remote could otherwise plant one.
.PP
Recovery deliberately needs nothing but
.BR gpg (1),
.BR zstd (1)
and
.BR tar (1).
See
.B break\-glass
and
.I BREAK\-GLASS.md
in
.IR /usr/share/doc/timecrate/ .
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B init " [\fB\-\-new\-identity\fR]"
Generate the encryption and signing keypairs and print the escrow instructions. Run once. Safe to
re\-run: existing keys are kept.
.TP
.B backup " [\-\-dry\-run] [\-\-no\-upload] [\-\-keep\-staging] [\-\-force]"
Build a kit and upload it. Requires root.
.B \-\-dry\-run
prints the size and top\-level members and ships nothing.
.B \-\-force
overrides the size rails \(em see
.B SAFETY RAILS
below.
.TP
.B list " [\-\-long]"
List the kits present on the remote. Fails loudly if the remote cannot be read; an unreadable
remote is never reported as an empty one.
.B \-\-long
adds two columns \(em size in bytes and modification time \(em tab\-separated, before the name.
Bytes are raw rather than human\-formatted because this output is parsed by the graphical
front\-end; pipe through
.B numfmt \-\-to=iec \-\-field=1
for a friendlier column. Bare
.B list
stays name\-only: it is what
.B restore
validates its argument against.
.TP
.B restore " \fIts\fR [\-\-to\-root] [\-\-force] | \-\-local \fIfile\fR"
Fetch, verify and extract a kit. By default it extracts to a staging directory for inspection and
your running system is untouched.
.B \-\-to\-root
extracts straight to
.I /
for a bare\-metal restore.
.B \-\-force
is required to restore a kit older than the newest one recorded, which is otherwise refused as a
rollback.
.TP
.B verify " \fIfile\fR | \fB\-\-remote\fR [\fIts\fR] | \fB\-\-all\fR"
Check a kit end to end: checksum, signature, decryption and archive integrity.
.I file
is a kit already on this disk.
.B \-\-remote
fetches one from the remote first \(em the newest unless
.I ts
names another \(em and
.B \-\-all
does every kit there, one at a time so the whole remote is never on local disk at once. A sweep
does not stop at the first bad kit: it reports how many of how many verified and names the ones
that did not, because once one has failed the only useful question is how many others did.
Distinct from
.BR recovery\-drill ,
which proves the \fIescrowed\fR key against a kit; this proves the kits against the key installed
here.
.TP
.B status " [\-\-json]"
Report the live state: remote, kit count, last backup, key, escrow and last drill. Needs no root.
.TP
.B config
Print every effective setting and which file it came from. Run this first whenever the tool and
you disagree about how it is configured.
.TP
.B schedule " [\fIbackup\fR|\fIdrill\fR \fIon\fR|\fIoff\fR [\-\-at \fIOnCalendar\fR]]"
With no arguments, report whether each timer is enabled, when it runs and when it runs next.
Otherwise enable or disable it, optionally setting the schedule \(em
.BR daily ,
.BR monthly ,
.BR "*-*-* 03:00:00" .
An invalid calendar expression is refused rather than written: systemd does not reject one at
enable time, it simply never fires the timer again, which for a backup is indistinguishable from
working until the day you need a restore.
The package enables and starts the backup, drill and capstone timers when it is installed, and
an upgrade leaves each one as it was; the capstone is skipped on a machine without multipass.
.TP
.B recovery\-drill " [\fIkey\fR] [\fIts\fR]"
Prove recovery actually works. Imports only the escrowed key into a throwaway keyring \(em the
wiped\-OS scenario \(em fetches a real kit from the remote, and decrypts and authenticates it.
Also sweeps every kit on the remote for signature validity and checks the remote against the
recorded anchor for deletion or rollback.
.TP
.B demo
Self\-test the whole crypto and fidelity pipeline offline in a throwaway keyring, including a
forged\-kit refusal. No root, no network.
.TP
.B escrow\-doc " [\fB\-\-format\fR \fBtxt\fR|\fBpdf\fR] [\fIoutfile\fR]"
Write a printable recovery sheet holding the secret key, the signing public key, the
post\-quantum secret key when one exists, and the manual recovery steps, as plain text or as a
PDF (\fB\-\-format\fR, or a \fB.pdf\fR suffix). Refuses to write anywhere inside the backup set.
.TP
.B pq " \fBinit\fR|\fBstatus\fR [\fB\-\-json\fR]"
Manage the ML\-KEM\-1024 post\-quantum keypair. With
.B TIMECRATE_CRYPTO=hybrid
each new kit is the ordinary signed GPG kit wrapped in a PQ envelope whose shared secret keys a
symmetric AES\-256 layer; opening one needs both secrets. \fBinit\fR also runs it. Classic kits
stay readable either way.
.TP
.B escrow\-confirm
Record that the key is safely escrowed off this machine. Clears the backup warning.
.TP
.B harden
Delete the on\-box secret key and shred the on\-box escrow file, leaving the machine able to
encrypt but never decrypt. Irreversible, and gated on proving an off\-box copy exists first.
.TP
.B break\-glass " \fIfile\fR [\fItarget\fR]"
Print, and then run, the raw
.BR gpg / zstd / tar
recovery steps \(em recovery when
.B timecrate
itself is not available.
.TP
.B remote " \fIsubcommand\fR"
Manage where the backups go. Needs no privileges.
.RS
.TP
.B status " [\fB\-\-json\fR]"
Path, backend, account, token expiry and whether the remote is reachable right now. Never prints
the token itself.
.TP
.B connect " [\fB\-\-provider\fR \fIname\fR] [\fB\-\-reauthorize\fR]"
Run the provider's authorisation and store it in rclone's config. Detects a session with no
browser and prints the \fBrclone authorize\fR half of the dance instead of hanging.
\fB\-\-provider drive\fR is Google Drive direct sign\-in: its own rclone remote
(\fBTIMECRATE_DRIVE_REMOTE\fR, default \fBgdrive\fR) in its own config
(\fBTIMECRATE_DRIVE_CONF\fR), independent of the main rclone config; afterwards pick the backup
folder with \fBbrowse\fR and \fBset\-path\fR.
.TP
.B test
Prove the remote both lists and accepts writes, and name the reason when it does not: a missing
path, a rejected token, an unknown rclone remote and a read-only destination are four different
faults.
.TP
.B browse " [\fIremote:path\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR]"
One level of folders, for choosing one.
.TP
.B mkdir " \fIremote:path\fR"
.TP
.B set-path " \fIremote:path\fR [\fB\-\-yes\fR]"
Repoint future backups. Prints the backup count at both ends first and warns when the current path
holds backups the new one does not; they are neither moved nor deleted. The previous configuration
is kept alongside as \fI.bak\fR.
.TP
.B disconnect " [\fB\-\-remove\-rclone\-remote\fR] [\fB\-\-yes\fR]"
Forget the remote on this machine. Does not touch the backups and does not revoke access at the
provider.
.RE
.TP
.B keys " [\fB\-\-json\fR]\"
Report the encryption and signing keys, whether each secret half is present on this machine, and
whether the escrow has been confirmed \(em and for which key. Needs no privileges.
.TP
.B import\-key " \fIfile\fR|\fB\-\fR [\fB\-\-signing\-pub\fR \fIfile\fR] [\fB\-\-replace\fR] [\fB\-\-yes\fR] [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR]"
Import an escrowed secret key onto a rebuilt machine. The key is validated in a throwaway keyring
before the real one is touched: it must be RSA on its encryption\-capable part, carry a secret
half, and not be expired or revoked.
.B \-\-dry\-run
validates and reports the fingerprint without importing.
.B \-\-replace
re\-points new backups at the imported key while keeping the superseded secret, so archives
encrypted to it stay readable.
.TP
.B verify\-key " [\fB\-\-deep\fR]"
Answer whether \fIthis\fR machine can read the newest archive on the remote, by matching its
recipient key\-id against the local keyring.
.B \-\-deep
fetches and fully decrypts it, with signature verification mandatory. Distinct from
.BR "recovery\-drill" ,
which proves the \fIescrowed\fR copy works: a reinstalled machine can pass that and still be
unable to restore.
.TP
.B export\-key " [\fB\-\-out\fR \fIfile\fR]"
Write the secret key out for escrow. Refuses any path inside the backup include set \(em escrowing
the key into the archives it decrypts is the failure escrow exists to avoid.
.TP
.B paths " \fIinclude\fR|\fIexclude\fR [\fBlist\fR|\fBadd\fR \fIpath\fR|\fBremove\fR \fIpath\fR]"
Show or change what a kit contains.
.B list
needs no privileges;
.B add
and
.B remove
edit root\-owned files in /etc and do. An absolute path is stored relative to \fI/\fR, and one
under the configured user's home as \fI~/\fR so the list survives a username change. A path
containing a newline or \fI..\fR is refused.
.TP
.B history " [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-n\fR \fIcount\fR]"
What this machine has been doing: backups and drills, with size and duration where they were
recorded. Lines written before v1.9.0 carry neither and report as not recorded rather than as zero.
.TP
.B alerts " [\fB\-\-json\fR]"
Whether a failed backup or drill would reach anyone: which command it runs, or that it is written
to the journal only. The command may be set in the root\-only secrets file, so an unprivileged run
that cannot read it reports that it cannot tell rather than that nothing is configured.
.TP
.B alerts test
Send a real alert down the same path, and at the same severity, as a failure. Needs root.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Settings are read from four sources. Strongest first:
.IP 1. 4
The environment \(em
.B TIMECRATE_REMOTE=... timecrate list
.IP 2. 4
.I ~/.config/timecrate/timecrate.conf
.IP 3. 4
.I /etc/timecrate.env
\(em mode 0600, root only, for secrets
.IP 4. 4
.I /etc/timecrate/timecrate.conf
.PP
Every entry point reads all four, including the systemd timers and the GUI. Each setting is a
shell assignment named
.BI TIMECRATE_ NAME\fR.
.B timecrate config
prints the resolved values with their source.
.SH ALERTS
A failed backup, drill or capstone is always written to the system journal. To be told anywhere
else, set
.B TIMECRATE_ALERT_CMD
to an executable. Every alert runs it as
.PP
.RS
.I command severity title key
.RE
.PP
with the message on standard input.
.I severity
is
.BR critical ,
.B warn
or
.BR info ;
.I title
is one line;
.I key
names the kind of alert
.RB ( backup ", " remote ", " drill ", " capstone ", " overnight ", " unit\-\fIname\fR, " test )
so a receiver can update one message in place instead of adding another. It runs as root, with
.I /etc/timecrate.env
in its environment \(em the place for any token it needs. Exit status 0 means delivered; anything
else is reported as a failed delivery, and makes
.B alerts test
fail. A wrapper script is the way to pass arguments.
.SH OFF\-BOX ESCROW
.B TIMECRATE_ESCROW_KEY_CMD
names an executable that prints the escrowed secret key, ASCII\-armored, on standard output \(em
from a password manager's command\-line tool or a secret store. It runs as root with
.I /etc/timecrate.env
in its environment. The monthly drill decrypts with the key it prints, which proves the copy that
survives a wiped machine rather than the one on it, and
.B harden
compares its fingerprint before shredding anything.
.B TIMECRATE_ESCROW_SSH
is the other route: a host holding a copy of the escrow file, compared by hash.
.SH KITS FROM EARLIER VERSIONS
Releases before 3.0.0 named kits
.IR timemachine\-<timestamp>.tar.zst.gpg ,
with a
.I TIMEMACHINE\-MANIFESTS
directory inside. Every command reads them: they are listed, verified, restored, drilled and
pruned together with
.I timecrate\-*
kits, as one series ordered by timestamp. Nothing needs renaming. See
.I MIGRATING.md
for moving a machine from the previous package names.
.SH SAFETY RAILS
.TP
.B Size cap and floor
A kit larger than
.B TIMECRATE_MAX_MB
means an include went wrong; one smaller than
.B TIMECRATE_MIN_MB
means the include set collapsed and the kit is hollow. Either refuses to ship, because a hollow
kit would upload clean and then age out the real ones.
.TP
.B Signature before extraction
Kits are materialised and verified before anything is extracted, never streamed through
.BR tar .
.TP
.B Anti\-rollback
Each upload records the expected newest kit and count. Restoring anything older needs
.BR \-\-force ,
and the backup and drill both alert on unexpected deletion or rollback on the remote \(em an old
kit carries a perfectly valid signature, so signing alone cannot catch this.
.TP
.B Automatic exclusions
The keyring and escrow file, the rclone token and the staging directory are excluded from every
kit, derived from live configuration. Editing the exclude file cannot re\-admit them.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/timecrate/timecrate.conf
System configuration.
.TP
.I /etc/timecrate/include
Paths that make up the kit, relative to
.IR / .
.B ~/
expands to the configured user's home.
.TP
.I /etc/timecrate/exclude
Patterns pruned from inside the included paths.
.TP
.I /etc/timecrate.env
Root\-only secrets, mode 0600. The systemd units load it too.
.TP
.I ~/.config/timecrate/
Keyring, escrow file and pinned signing fingerprint.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/timecrate/status.json
Machine\-readable status, consumed by
.B status
and the GUI. Fields are only ever added, never renamed or removed. Size fields
.RB ( cloud_bytes ", " remote_quota_used ", " remote_quota_total ", " last_backup_bytes ", "
.BR previous_backup_bytes )
are strings, and an empty string means
.I not measured
\(em never zero, because a remote that could not be read and a remote that is genuinely empty
must not render alike.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/timecrate/log
One line per run. A backup line is
.IR "<iso\-timestamp> ok <kit\-timestamp> <filename> <bytes> <seconds>" .
The last two fields were added in 1.9.0; lines written by older versions have four fields and
are read as \(lqsize not recorded\(rq. A
.B \-
in either position means this run could not measure it.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero on success. Any failure exits non\-zero with a line prefixed
.BR [timecrate][ERROR] .
Warnings that do not stop the run are prefixed
.BR [timecrate][WARN] .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR timecrate\-gui (1),
.BR rclone (1),
.BR gpg (1),
.BR zstd (1),
.BR tar (1)
.PP
.IR RESTORE.md ,
.I BREAK\-GLASS.md
and
.I MIGRATING.md
in
.IR /usr/share/doc/timecrate/ ,
and https://github.com/spencercnorton/timecrate
.SH BUGS
The disaster this tool exists for is the one you cannot rehearse by reading. Run
.B recovery\-drill
on a schedule and treat a failure as an outage.
